<% set ev = targets.evaluation_sets %>
<% set P3 = "Part 3 — Objectives and measurable success criteria" %>
<% set P4 = "Part 4 — Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics" %>
<% set needs = {"api_key": "†", "hardware": "‡", "team_suite": "§"} %>
*Executive summary.* Analog audio equipment (tube preamplifiers, studio consoles, vintage hi-fi) is kept running by bench technicians, and finding the failed part is the skilled, separately billed step: specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>. Differential assists a qualified technician: for a modelled circuit (@fig:concept) it simulates every catalogued single-part fault under part tolerances, keeps a probability for every suspect, and recommends and explains the measurement expected to rule out the most suspects per unit of effort. A language model reads complaints and explains, but never supplies a reading the technician has not confirmed; safety rules run in code. Seven primary targets, locked before the full evaluation (@tab:targets), test the claims: the failed part or its group of indistinguishable faults named first in at least << pct(target('T1').value, 0) >> of << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> simulated units; at least << (100 * target('T4').value) | round | int >> points more accurate than a scripted chart and than random probing, and << (100 * target('T5').value) | round | int >> more than a language model alone; at most << target('T8').value >> times a chart's measurement effort; calibrated probabilities; and no broken safety or grounding rule. Scope: single static faults in preamplifier and line-level circuits.

