<< part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context") >>
<% set cs = m('library.circuits.channel_strip') %>
<% set hv = m('hv_audit.circuits.channel_strip') %>

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi often outlive their makers. When one fails, a technician must find which of dozens to hundreds of parts failed; that search, more than the repair, takes the skill and the bench time. Differential is an assistant for that search.

*Diagnosis is billed on its own.* Three specialist shops we sampled (studio, vintage hi-fi, guitar and tube amplifier) charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation, at one covering the first hour of work, at another credited to the repair << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>; a studio-gear shop bills a one-hour minimum even when no fault is found << cite('rates_british_audio') >>. These are single-shop examples: the share of a bill that diagnosis takes is not published, so we will ask technicians before M2.

*A workforce that is hard to replace.* The closest U.S. occupation, audiovisual equipment installers and repairers, covers installation as well as repair; it employed 24,720 people in May 2023 << cite('bls_oews_492097_2023') >> and is projected to grow faster than average << cite('onet_492097_bright_outlook') >>. Vintage analog repair relies on a narrower pool of bench-trained specialists that formal programmes rarely produce; many are near retirement and repair queues run to months or years << cite('headphonesty_vintage_techs_2026') >>, and one trade column reports that analog circuitry is not taught at two-year colleges << cite('stereophile_retales_13') >>. An AI assistant raised the productivity of 5,179 customer-support agents by 14% on average and 34% for novices << cite('brynjolfsson_genai_at_work_2023') >>; if that pattern holds at the bench, newly qualified technicians would gain most. Parts wear in known ways, electrolytic capacitors losing capacitance and gaining series resistance as their electrolyte evaporates << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and tubes have been scarce since early 2022 << cite('headphonesty_tube_shortage_2025') >>, so replacing good parts on a guess is costly.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI retrieves documented knowledge for instrumented fleets and common devices: PTC's ServiceMax AI (2025) << cite('ptc_servicemax_ai_2025') >>, Microsoft's field-service Copilot << cite('msft_d365_field_service_page') >>, Siemens's repair guidance (2025) << cite('siemens_maintenance_copilot_2025') >>, IBM Maximo 9.2's agentic assistant (June 2026) << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >> and iFixit's FixBot (2025), drawing on 125,000 repair guides << cite('ifixit_fixbot_2025') >>. None of these models the circuit of a unit without sensors. At the bench the alternatives are the service manual's voltage chart, the unit's healthy second channel, power-off signature testers, forums and chat models. The best chat model scores 95.50% on the September 2026 Razavi-Bench of textbook analog questions, as judged by other models << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>, but a unit on the bench differs: parts sit anywhere within tolerance, different faults give the same readings, measurements cost effort, and some points carry lethal voltage. One system reached 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems after adding a simulation loop that flags mismatches << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >>; Differential is built around such a check. @tab:stakeholders lists who is affected.

