#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant, or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional, because diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis. Under OSHA's electrical rules, our benchmark, live parts are de-energised before work unless testing needs power, parts under 50 V to ground are exempt, and only qualified persons may work on parts that are not de-energised << cite('osha_1910_333') >> or test circuits << cite('osha_1910_334') >>. Our audit of every simulated unit found that << hv.hv_normal + hv.hv_under_fault >> of the channel strip's << cs.test_points >> test points can exceed 50 V, << hv.hv_normal >> in normal operation and << hv.hv_under_fault >> only under a fault (<< P4 >>). *Consequence:* the interface assumes meter and oscilloscope skills, mains-side work is refused, and the tool will be offered to repair shops, not consumers.
]

<< part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape") >>

*Today's practice.* The usual sequence, which our interviews will check, is to confirm the complaint, look for burnt or bulging parts and check the supplies; follow a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-splitting"); compare voltages with the service manual's chart or the healthy channel; swap tubes; and finally unsolder a suspect part to test it alone. What varies is judgement about which reading is really "off", since every part sits somewhere within its tolerance and old parts drift beyond it. When judgement runs out, a common fallback is replacing every electrolytic capacitor ("recapping"), sometimes justified by age << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, sometimes a substitute for diagnosis.

*The information is uneven.* Service manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. State repair laws require documentation only for digital products: in New York since 28 December 2023 << cite('ny_ag_repair_2024') >>, for products first sold from 1 July 2023 << cite('ny_gbl_399nn') >>, and in Texas since 1 September 2026 << cite('tx_hb2963_2025') >>. The EU's obligation to repair covers only product groups in its Annex II, such as washing machines and displays << cite('eu_r2r_directive_2024_1799', 'ec_r2r_qa_2024') >>. Vintage analog gear falls outside all of these.

*What Differential needs from a unit.* A circuit model: the netlist (the parts and how they connect), where each test point is, and the operating conditions, taken from the service-manual schematic; M2 will time one transcription. Units modified away from their schematic are what the "no single fault fits" outcome is for; units with no schematic are out of scope.

*Labelled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded in structured form, shop records are private, and each model is a different circuit (@tab:data). We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published fault-diagnosis methods are evaluated on benchmark filter circuits << cite('gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >> and simulated data << cite('cloete_stander_wilke_2022_access') >>. The analog-test literature built simulated fault dictionaries << cite('bandler_salama_1985') >>, showed that some faults cannot be told apart at the available test points, forming ambiguity groups << cite('stenbakken_souders_stewart_1989') >>, and later applied machine learning << cite('he_he_li_2020_gan_tim', 'gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >> to drift faults that normal tolerance variation makes hard to detect << cite('cloete_stander_wilke_2022_access') >>; we follow that path. A simulated unit is one draw of every part within its datasheet tolerance plus one catalogued fault: an open, a short, a value drift, or a part-specific mode such as a leaky capacitor or a worn tube. Five original blocks and a composite channel strip (<< cs.components >> parts, << cs.faults >> faults) gave << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> simulated units with << m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> convergence failures. Hand calculations agree with every simulated bias point and gain within stated tolerances of << num(m('hand_calcs.rel_tol_min_pct'), 1) >>-<< num(m('hand_calcs.rel_tol_max_pct'), 0) >>% or << num(m('hand_calcs.abs_tol_db'), 1) >> dB (<< m('hand_calcs.rows_ok') >> of << m('hand_calcs.rows_checked') >> checks), and checking fault behaviour by hand caught one simulator artifact, since fixed: an open cathode resistor read half the plate voltage through a leakage resistor in the tube model. A complaint alone is weak evidence: on the channel strip, a hum complaint fits << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.hum') >> faults and low output << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.low_gain') >>, and << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.latent_faults') >> of the << cs.faults >> faults cause no symptom in most units.

