*How it will be measured.* The test set holds << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> single-fault units (<< ev.composite_cases >> on the channel strip, << ev.cases_per_block >> per block), sampled uniformly from simulated units that show a symptom, so each fault appears as often as it would reach a bench; seeds are disjoint from training. Each unit gets a templated complaint in one of three voices (owner, musician, technician) that never names a part; a separately written paraphrase bank tests the complaint reader. Readings carry meter error of ±(<< num(m('library.instrument.dmm_pct'), 1) >>% + << m('library.instrument.dmm_counts') >> counts) and oscilloscope error of ±<< num(m('library.instrument.scope_pct'), 0) >>%; meter loading is not modelled. Further sets: << ev.unmodeled_cases >> double-fault or modified units, and << ev.stress_cases >> units with tolerances << ev.stress_tol_scale >> times wider. Every unit is diagnosed by random probing, a fixed-order chart, half-split tracing, the engine with each of two likelihood models, and the full system. On a stratified << ev.llm_subset >>-unit subset a language model works alone, and with our simulator as a tool (reported without a bar), seeing what a technician sees (complaint, parts list, voltage chart, fault list, readings on request, the same budget), with a pinned model and a published prompt. Also reported: accuracy by how often a fault causes a symptom; group sizes (on the channel strip, << m('library.groups.channel_strip.hypotheses') >> hypotheses form << m('library.groups.channel_strip.groups') >> groups, << m('library.groups.channel_strip.singletons') >> of size one); and misses split into wrong names and "no single fault fits" calls. A study of technicians using the tool on the fault board, with and without it, is planned for M3 and not locked.

*Pilots set the bars; the test set was not touched.* Two earlier pilots exposed problems, since fixed: the "no single fault fits" threshold was not yet calibrated, so the first (engine alone) scored << pct(m('pilot.circuits.channel_strip.eig_per_cost.top1'), 0) >> on the channel strip without ever using that outcome; and in the second, measurement choice ignored that outcome, so some sessions stopped early. The bars come from a third pilot, run after the fixes on units simulated for it alone (<< m('pilot2.n_cases.channel_strip') >> channel strip, << m('pilot2.n_cases.driver') >> per block). On the channel strip the full system named the group in << pct(m('pilot2.channel_strip.hybrid.top1'), 1) >> of units at a mean effort of << num(m('pilot2.channel_strip.hybrid.mean_cost'), 1) >>, against << pct(m('pilot2.channel_strip.fixed_order.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('pilot2.channel_strip.fixed_order.mean_cost'), 1) >> for the chart, << pct(m('pilot2.channel_strip.half_split.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('pilot2.channel_strip.half_split.mean_cost'), 1) >> for half-split tracing and << pct(m('pilot2.channel_strip.random.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('pilot2.channel_strip.random.mean_cost'), 1) >> for random probing, and << pct(m('pilot2.wide_top1'), 1) >> with wider tolerances. The "no single fault fits" threshold is set on validation units to flag at most << pct(m('library.design.false_alarm_budget'), 0) >> of single faults; << m('pilot2.hybrid_false_flags') >> of the full system's << m('pilot2.hybrid_misses') >> misses over all << m('pilot2.n_total') >> pilot units were such calls, the price of flagging << pct(m('pilot2.hybrid_unmodeled_flag_rate'), 0) >> of double faults and modified units.

*Scope and non-goals.* In scope: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause. Out of scope: power stages (cascading and mains-side faults need another catalog); intermittent and mechanical faults and random noise such as crackle (a static simulation cannot produce them); double faults (flagged, not diagnosed); mains-side work (safety); units without a schematic (no model); probing without a technician.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and weighted effort")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or accuracy by ambiguity group and weighted effort. *Choice:* the latter. Some faults give identical readings at every test point; scoring per part would reward lucky guesses and punish honest ties, and counting measurements would treat unsoldering a part like touching a probe. *Consequence:* accuracy targets are group aware, group sizes are reported, and effort targets use weighted units.
]

<< part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics") >>

*Why a hybrid.* Language models sometimes guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing over admitting uncertainty << cite('kalai_why_hallucinate_2025') >>, and the 97.59% system above relied on a simulation check. So simulation supplies the physics, and Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect, "no single fault fits" included, updated after each reading. The next measurement is the one with the highest expected information gain per unit of effort, as in sequential fault diagnosis << cite('pattipati_alexandridis_1990') >> and analog test selection << cite('bandler_salama_1985') >>; a diagnosis stops when one group reaches << pct(m('library.design.stop_threshold'), 0) >> probability, the budget runs out, or no measurement is informative. The LLM reads complaints (with a rule-based offline fallback), proposes meter-photo readings for the technician to confirm, and explains each step from the engine's outputs; every number it writes must trace to a measurement or tool result (T17). @tab:families compares the options.

