*Autonomy.* On a five-level scale borrowed from driving automation, from L0 (manual) to L4 (the tool probes alone), this build works at L2: it recommends; the technician measures and decides. Only on the 24 V fault board does it read instruments, over SCPI and with approval (L3); L4 is a non-goal.

*Safety.* A fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: in our audit of every simulated unit, channel-strip test point << hv.showcase.tp >>, normally at about << num(hv.showcase.normal_max_v, 0) >> V DC, reaches << num(hv.showcase.worst_case_v, 0) >> V under the fault "<< hv.showcase.fault_label >>". Steps at any point that can exceed 50 V carry hands-off measurement and discharge instructions; in a chassis with a high-voltage supply every powered step carries a live-chassis notice; unsoldering stays locked until the technician enters a measured filter-capacitor voltage below << num(m('library.design.discharge_verify_max_v'), 0) >> V; mains-side work is refused. A step is unsafe if it bypasses a protection, directs hands-in work on a live or undischarged circuit, or omits these instructions (T15, T16). These guarantees cover the modelled circuit only; a modified or mis-transcribed unit may differ, hence the whole-chassis notice.

*Over-trust and bias.* Runner-up suspects and each step's reason are always shown and every ticket needs the technician's sign-off, but explanations can also raise trust in wrong advice; the technician study will include a deliberately wrong recommendation, and sampled explanations will be checked against the engine's actual reasons. Old electrolytics do fail often, but folklore blames them first; T14 tests that readings override a capacitor-heavy prior. Errors will be broken down by circuit, part kind and fault type, after the disaggregated audit of commercial gender classifiers << cite('buolamwini_gebru_2018') >>. Complaint reading is scored by writer voice, but the voices are our templates; non-native writers and regional terms ("valve", "earth") stay untested until the team collects real complaints.

*Security and privacy.* Prompt injection ranks first in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications << cite('owasp_llm_top10_2026') >>, and LLM-integrated applications blur data and instructions << cite('greshake_2023') >>; service notes, complaints and photo text are treated as data, and the model's tools are read-only. Offline mode sends nothing out; live mode sends complaint text and metadata-free meter photos to the model provider.

*Accountability, liability and regulation.* The tool would first go to U.S. repair shops; EU law, like OSHA, serves as a benchmark. Responsibility is allocated, not signed away: the team answers for the hazard map, stated performance, warnings and updates; the shop for who uses the tool and for its circuit models; the technician for the measurements and precautions taken. U.S. product-liability and negligence law already applies; the proposed RISE Act would shield developers who publish a model card and specification, but only when a "learned professional" uses the tool << cite('us_rise_act_s2081') >>. A bench technician may not qualify, so we publish both for transparency, not as a shield. The revised EU Product Liability Directive counts software as a product from 9 December 2026 and excludes damage to property used only professionally, not personal injury, the harm that matters here << cite('eu_pld_2024_2853') >>. The EU AI Act would not class a bench aid as high-risk, as it is neither, nor a safety component of, a product that Annex I law subjects to third-party conformity assessment, nor in an Annex III area; but Annex III covers evaluating workers' performance << cite('eu_ai_act_text_mirror') >>, so our intended use excludes grading technicians. From 2 August 2026 people must be told they are dealing with an AI unless it is obvious << cite('eu_ai_act_text_mirror', 'ec_ip_26_1714') >>. @tab:risks maps controls to NIST AI RMF functions << cite('nist_ai_rmf_1_0') >>.

